bool solveMaze(vector<string> g) {
    int n = g.size(), m = g[0].size(), goods = 0;
    int dr[4] = {1, -1, 0, 0}, dc[4] = {0, 0, 1, -1};
    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
        for (int c = 0; c < m; c++) {
            if (g[r][c] == 'G') goods++;
            if (g[r][c] != 'B') continue;
            for (int t = 0; t < 4; t++) {
                int nr = r + dr[t], nc = c + dc[t];
                if (nr < 0 || nc < 0 || nr >= n || nc >= m) continue;
                if (g[nr][nc] == 'G') return false;
                if (g[nr][nc] == '.') g[nr][nc] = '#';
            }
        }
    }
    if (!goods) return true;
    if (g[n - 1][m - 1] == '#') return false;
    queue<pair<int, int>> q;
    vector<vector<char>> seen(n, vector<char>(m));
    q.push({n - 1, m - 1});
    seen[n - 1][m - 1] = 1;
    int reached = 0;
    while (!q.empty()) {
        auto [r, c] = q.front();
        q.pop();
        if (g[r][c] == 'G') reached++;
        if (g[r][c] == 'B') return false;
        for (int t = 0; t < 4; t++) {
            int nr = r + dr[t], nc = c + dc[t];
            if (nr < 0 || nc < 0 || nr >= n || nc >= m || seen[nr][nc] || g[nr][nc] == '#')
                continue;
            seen[nr][nc] = 1;
            q.push({nr, nc});
        }
    }
    return reached == goods;
}
```

:::

:::

**Worked trace.** `#B` / `G.` puts `B` next to the exit. Sealing walls the exit, a good person remains, and the answer is no. `#B.` / `#..` / `GG.` walls the two empties beside `B`. The exit stays `.`, and both `G` cells are still joined to it. `#B` / `B.` has no good person, so walling the exit is allowed and the answer is yes.

**Edge cases.** A single empty cell is yes. A `B` diagonally touching a `G` is not adjacent: four-connectivity leaves them separable. The statement keeps the exit empty at the start, so a `G` is never already standing on it.

**Pitfalls.** Walling the `B` cell itself is forbidden, and it is unnecessary: walling its empty neighbours is the seal. Searching from the goods before the seal lets a good person walk through the cell that should have become a wall. Answering yes as soon as the exit is reachable, without counting the goods, ignores a `G` shut in a corner.


::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- With no good person the answer is yes, even when the seal walls the exit. `#B` / `B.` is that case. Answering no there fails the sample.
- Searching from every `G` is correct and too slow: one search from the exit reaches them all.
- Walling only a shortest path from `B` to the exit leaves another route open. The empty neighbours of `B` are the cells the seal may use.
- Treating `G` as a wall misses a good person who is reached only through another `G`.
:::

**Variants.** Several exits are one search that starts from all of them. Cells that cannot reach the border are the surrounded-regions exercise: search from the border and flip what it never marks. A corridor with a direction is the same check on the reversed arcs.

Source: [CF 1365D · Solve The Maze](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1365/D){.solved rating="1700"}


**Looking back.** Five patterns, one recursive call. Every answer was written at one of its moments — while scanning the neighbours, on entry, along a tree edge, on exit, or on the way back out — or by starting the same call from the other end. The colours and the timestamps are simply the record of which moment each vertex has reached, and that record is what turns a walk into an algorithm.

## Pattern map

| When the statement looks like this | Pattern | First move |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Print a cycle, or decide that a digraph is a DAG | Back edge | Gray means the root path; skip the parent on an undirected edge |
| A sum, size or count on every subtree | Exit | Children return first. Parents with smaller indices: loop from $n$ down to $2$ |
| The $k$-th vertex under $u$, or descendants at a depth | Times | One preorder. The subtree is an interval |
| All simple paths, or a best path on a tiny grid | Backtracking | Push, recurse, restore |
| Who can reach the exit, or the ancestors of $v$ | Reverse | Search from the target on the reversed arcs |

## Pitfalls

- The parent of an undirected search is the tree edge seen from the child. Counting it builds a cycle of length $2$ on every tree.
- A directed edge into a black vertex is forward or cross. The cycle test is "the head is gray".
- Subtree sums move from the leaves toward the root. A loop from $2$ up to $n$ adds a vertex before its children have arrived, and the sample then reports $1$ balanced subtree.
- The index loop for sizes needs every parent index smaller than the child. A LeetCode parent array may point at a larger index, and the product is a $64$-bit integer.
- A pending addition is pushed down a parent-before-child order. Recursion on the path of length $2 \cdot 10^5$ is the case that order replaces.
- $[\mathrm{tin}(u), \mathrm{tout}(u)]$ includes $u$. A query whose depth equals $\mathrm{dep}[u]$ counts $u$.
- On a DAG the path stack needs no visited array. On a grid the mark comes off on the way out.
- Ancestors are the vertices that reach $v$. The search runs on the reversed arcs and leaves $v$ itself out of the list.
- A good person standing next to a bad person cannot be separated by walls. With no good person, walling the exit is allowed.

